        "findings": "VERDICT: CHANGES REQUESTED\n\nThe change is functionally coherent and the javadocs are excellent, but two structural findings cut against this codebase's own strongly-established idiom. This repo consistently centralizes any rule shared by a write-time and a read-time side into a \"single source of truth\" helper (`OwnerLocality`, `OwnerTelephone`, `OwnerIdentity`, `OwnerEmail`), each with a javadoc that explicitly says it exists so the two sides \"cannot drift.\" The diff introduces two places that violate that pattern:\n\n1. **The `customerCode` format contract is split across two packages.** `AssignCustomerCode` *constructs* `<REGION>-<HASH8>` (`regionCode + \"-\" + hash8(...)`), while `OwnerMapper.locality` *parses* it back ad-hoc (`indexOf('-')` / `substring(0, dash)`) to recover the region. That is exactly the write-time/read-time coupling the codebase centralizes elsewhere \u2014 and `locality`'s own new javadoc claims \"the region therefore cannot drift from the identity,\" yet the format knowledge is now duplicated in `rest.function.owner` and in `mapper`, so a change to the separator or layout requires editing both in lockstep. Following the existing template (`OwnerIdentity.of` / `.key`), lift the format into one helper exposing both construction and a `regionOf(customerCode)` parse, and have both sites go through it. That would also naturally absorb the now-duplicated null-region\u2192`\"UNKNOWN\"` mapping (`AssignCustomerCode:33-34` and `OwnerMapper:120-122`).\n\n2. **`AssignCustomerCode.normalizedTelephone` (lines 38-42) is a character-for-character copy of `OwnerIdentity.normalizeTelephone` (lines 48-51).** The \"canonical E.164, or the raw value when it cannot form one\" fallback is a specific policy on top of `OwnerTelephone.toE164`, and it now lives in two classes. Given `OwnerTelephone` is documented as the single source of truth for telephone normalisation, this fallback variant belongs there (e.g. a `toE164OrRaw(...)` method) and should be called from both sites rather than reimplemented.\n\nNeither is a correctness problem; both are duplication / misplaced-logic issues that a maintainer of this particular codebase would flag because the surrounding code so deliberately avoids them."
      }
    ]
  }
}